<LegalLayout
  title="Patient Rights & Responsibilities"
  subtitle="Our commitment to the Charter of Patients' Rights notified by the Ministry of Health & Family Welfare, Government of India."
  updated="September 2026"
>
  <h2>Our Commitment</h2>
  <p>
    {hospital.name} is committed to delivering care that is safe, respectful, dignified and free from discrimination.
    This page summarises the <strong>Charter of Patients' Rights</strong> issued by the Ministry of Health &amp; Family
    Welfare (MoHFW), Government of India, and the responsibilities we ask of patients and their families.
  </p>

  <h2>Your Rights as a Patient</h2>

  <h3>1. Right to Information</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>You have the right to receive complete, accurate and understandable information about your diagnosis, proposed treatment, expected outcomes, risks and alternatives.</li>
    <li>You have the right to know the name and qualification of the doctor(s) treating you.</li>
    <li>You have the right to receive an itemised bill and an estimate of the expected cost of treatment.</li>
    <li>You have the right to access your medical records and to a copy of your discharge summary.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Right to Records</h3>
  <p>
    You or your authorised representative may request copies of your clinical records. Requests are processed within a
    reasonable time after identity verification, and applicable copying charges, if any, are disclosed in advance.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Right to Emergency Care</h3>
  <p>
    Under law, no hospital may refuse emergency medical treatment on any ground, including the ability to pay. Our
    emergency department operates <strong>24 × 7</strong> and will stabilise every patient who presents with an emergency.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Right to Informed Consent</h3>
  <p>
    No investigation or treatment will be carried out without your informed consent, except in a life-threatening
    emergency where delay would be harmful. You have the right to refuse treatment and to be informed of the consequences
    of that decision. For minors and patients unable to consent, consent is taken from a parent, guardian or legal
    representative.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Right to Confidentiality and Privacy</h3>
  <p>
    Your medical information and records are kept confidential. It is shared only with those directly involved in your
    care, when you authorise it, or where the law requires disclosure. You have the right to a private consultation
    environment and to have your dignity protected during examination and procedures.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Right to Second Opinion</h3>
  <p>
    You have the right to seek a second opinion from another qualified practitioner and to request that copies of relevant
    records be made available for that purpose.
  </p>

  <h3>7. Right to Transparency in Charges &amp; Estimates</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>We provide an estimated cost of planned admissions, surgeries and packages in advance where possible.</li>
    <li>You are entitled to know the breakdown of charges before incurring them.</li>
    <li>Cashless insurance and TPA arrangements, where applicable, are explained at the time of admission.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>8. Right to Dignity and Non-Discrimination</h3>
  <p>
    Care is provided without discrimination on the basis of religion, caste, gender, sexual orientation, disability,
    language, nationality or ability to pay. You have the right to be treated with courtesy and to have your cultural and
    religious sensitivities respected.
  </p>

  <h3>9. Right to Grievance Redressal</h3>
  <p>
    You have the right to raise a complaint about your care or experience and to receive a timely, documented response.
    See our <a href="/grievance">Grievance Redressal</a> page for the process, timelines and escalation matrix.
  </p>

  <h3>10. Right to Respect in Communication</h3>
  <p>
    You have the right to receive information in a language and manner you understand, including the support of a
    translator or attendant where reasonably possible.
  </p>

  <h2>Patient &amp; Family Responsibilities</h2>
  <p>To help us provide the safest possible care, we ask you to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Provide complete and accurate information about your symptoms, past illnesses, medications, allergies and previous treatments.</li>
    <li>Follow the treatment plan and medication instructions agreed with your care team, and inform the team of any change in your condition.</li>
    <li>Respect the hospital's staff, other patients and visitors, and observe visiting hours, silence zones and infection-control rules.</li>
    <li>Keep appointments punctually, and inform us in advance if you must reschedule or cancel.</li>
    <li>Take reasonable care of your belongings and of hospital property.</li>
    <li>Meet your financial obligations for services received, and keep your insurance documents ready where applicable.</li>
    <li>Follow the no-smoking, infection-control and medication-safety rules of the hospital.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How to Raise a Concern</h2>
  <p>
    You may raise a concern directly with any staff member, the nursing station, the treating doctor, or our
    <strong>Grievance Officer</strong>:
  </p>
  <p>
    Grievance Officer — {hospital.name}<br />
    {hospital.address.line1}, {hospital.address.line2}, {hospital.address.city}, {hospital.address.state} — {hospital.address.pincode}<br />
    Email: {hospital.email}<br />
    Phone: {hospital.phones.reception}
  </p>

  <p class="mt-6 text-sm">
    This charter is based on the Charter of Patients' Rights notified by the Ministry of Health &amp; Family Welfare,
    Government of India, and the National Accreditation Board for Hospitals &amp; Healthcare Providers (NABH) standards.
  </p>
</LegalLayout>
